NNAAMMEE
     ccffuummaassss — USB device side support for Mass Storage Class Transport

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     This driver can be compiled into the kernel by placing these lines in the
     kernel configuration file:

           ddeevviiccee uussbb
           ddeevviiccee uussbb__tteemmppllaattee
           ddeevviiccee ccttll
           ddeevviiccee ccffuummaassss

     The driver module can also be loaded at boot by adding this line to
     loader.conf(5):

           cfumass_load="YES"

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ccffuummaassss driver provides device side support for emulating an USB mass
     storage device compliant with the USB Mass Storage Class Bulk-Only (BBB)
     Transport specification, implemented as a ctl(4) frontend driver.

     To use ccffuummaassss:

     ••   ccffuummaassss must be loaded as a module or compiled into the kernel.

     ••   The USB Mass Storage template must be chosen by setting the
         _h_w_._u_s_b_._t_e_m_p_l_a_t_e sysctl to 0.

     ••   The USB OTG port must be working in USB device-side mode.  This hap‐
         pens automatically upon connection to a USB host.

     ••   There must be a ctl(4) LUN configured for the _c_f_u_m_a_s_s port.

     Upon loading, the driver creates a ctl(4) port named _c_f_u_m_a_s_s, presenting
     the first LUN mapped for that port - usually LUN 0 - to the USB host.
     See ctl.conf(5) and ctld(8) for details on configuring the LUN.  See the
     ccffuummaassss__eennaabbllee and ccffuummaassss__ddiirr rc(8) variables in rc.conf(5) for an auto‐
     mated way to configure it at boot.

SSYYSSCCTTLL VVAARRIIAABBLLEESS
     These variables are available as both sysctl(8) variables and loader(8)
     tunables:

     _h_w_._u_s_b_._c_f_u_m_a_s_s_._d_e_b_u_g
             Verbosity level for log messages from the ccffuummaassss driver.  Set to
             0 to disable logging or 1 to warn about potential problems.
             Larger values enable debugging output.  Defaults to 1.

     _h_w_._u_s_b_._c_f_u_m_a_s_s_._i_g_n_o_r_e___s_t_o_p
             Ignore START STOP UNIT SCSI commands with START and LOEJ bits
             cleared.  Some initiators send that command to stop the target
             when the user attempts to gracefully eject the drive, but fail to
             start it when the drive is reconnected.  Set to 0 to handle the
             command in a standards-compliant way, 1 to ignore it and log a
             warning, or 2 to ignore it silently.  Defaults to 1.

     _h_w_._u_s_b_._c_f_u_m_a_s_s_._m_a_x___l_u_n
             Max LUN number to report to the initiator (USB host).  Must be
             between 0 and 15.  Some initiators incorrectly handle values
             larger than 0.  Defaults to 0.

